1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,579
The police can search your car without a warrant.

2
00:00:02,580 --> 00:00:04,599
They've been able to for a hundred years —

3
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:08,279
with one exception.

4
00:00:08,280 --> 00:00:09,679
You get pulled over.

5
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:12,339
The officer walks up, glances through the glass,

6
00:00:12,340 --> 00:00:14,820
and says he's going to search the car.

7
00:00:15,580 --> 00:00:16,739
Can he?

8
00:00:16,740 --> 00:00:17,019
When?

9
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:20,279
And where, exactly, does that power run out?

10
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:22,459
The answer stretches from a Prohibition

11
00:00:22,460 --> 00:00:24,639
bootlegger on a Michigan highway to

12
00:00:24,640 --> 00:00:25,919
a motorcycle hidden under a

13
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:27,959
tarp in a Virginia driveway —

14
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:30,999
and it quietly decides how much privacy you

15
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:33,839
actually keep the moment you turn the key.

16
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:36,359
Because there is one place they still can't go —

17
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:39,119
and in 2018 the Supreme Court drew that

18
00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:43,539
line at the very edge of your home.

19
00:00:43,540 --> 00:00:44,679
Start with the thing

20
00:00:44,680 --> 00:00:46,859
that surprises almost everyone.

21
00:00:46,860 --> 00:00:47,879
To search your house,

22
00:00:47,880 --> 00:00:49,979
the police usually need a warrant —

23
00:00:49,980 --> 00:00:53,239
a judge's signature, probable cause written down,

24
00:00:53,240 --> 00:00:56,519
a name for the place and the thing they're after.

25
00:00:56,520 --> 00:00:59,939
That is the Fourth Amendment at full strength.

26
00:00:59,940 --> 00:01:01,779
Your car does not get that.

27
00:01:01,780 --> 00:01:04,199
And it hasn't since 1925 —

28
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:06,320
a hundred years ago.

29
00:01:07,520 --> 00:01:08,638
It was Prohibition.

30
00:01:08,640 --> 00:01:11,159
Alcohol was illegal,
and a whole underground economy ran

31
00:01:11,160 --> 00:01:13,319
it across state lines by car.

32
00:01:13,320 --> 00:01:14,519
A man named George Carroll

33
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:16,439
was one of those runners,

34
00:01:16,440 --> 00:01:18,159
moving liquor along the highway

35
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:20,799
between Detroit and Grand Rapids.

36
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:22,579
Federal agents had been watching him.

37
00:01:22,580 --> 00:01:23,779
They stopped his car,

38
00:01:23,780 --> 00:01:26,379
and they didn't find anything in plain sight —

39
00:01:26,380 --> 00:01:28,559
so they started tearing into it.

40
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:30,739
They ripped open the seat cushions.

41
00:01:30,740 --> 00:01:33,019
And hidden behind the upholstery of the seats,

42
00:01:33,020 --> 00:01:34,679
they pulled out sixty-eight bottles

43
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:37,059
of whisky and gin.

44
00:01:37,060 --> 00:01:40,319
Carroll's lawyers had what sounds
like a winning argument:

45
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:41,899
no warrant, no search.

46
00:01:41,900 --> 00:01:43,439
The agents had torn his car apart on the

47
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:45,919
side of the road with nothing on paper.

48
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:48,919
The case climbed all the way to the Supreme Court.

49
00:01:48,920 --> 00:01:50,679
And the Court gave an answer that

50
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:53,059
has governed your glovebox ever since.

51
00:01:53,060 --> 00:01:54,759
It drew a line —

52
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:58,200
not around what the police wanted,

53
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:00,979
but around the thing being searched.

54
00:02:00,980 --> 00:02:03,479
A house, the Court said, stays put.

55
00:02:03,480 --> 00:02:05,639
If officers have to leave and get a warrant,

56
00:02:05,640 --> 00:02:08,959
they can post someone at the door;

57
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:11,739
the house isn't going anywhere.
But a car is built to move.

58
00:02:11,740 --> 00:02:14,459
By the time a warrant is signed, a car —

59
00:02:14,460 --> 00:02:17,019
and whatever is hidden in its seats —

60
00:02:17,020 --> 00:02:20,539
can be in another county, another state, gone.

61
00:02:20,540 --> 00:02:22,481
Chief Justice Taft wrote that you

62
00:02:22,620 --> 00:02:24,159
can't treat the two the same,

63
00:02:24,160 --> 00:02:26,572
"because the vehicle can be quickly moved

64
00:02:26,960 --> 00:02:29,195
out of the locality or jurisdiction in

65
00:02:29,440 --> 00:02:30,919
which the warrant must be sought."

66
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:33,040
So the Court carved out what

67
00:02:33,840 --> 00:02:35,659
we now call the automobile exception.

68
00:02:35,660 --> 00:02:38,079
If police have probable cause to

69
00:02:38,080 --> 00:02:39,999
believe your car contains contraband,

70
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:41,759
they can search it —

71
00:02:41,760 --> 00:02:43,172
right there, no warrant.

72
00:02:43,540 --> 00:02:45,519
And this isn't a dusty relic.

73
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:47,379
That 1925 rule is still the rule

74
00:02:47,380 --> 00:02:50,199
behind why an ordinary traffic stop can,

75
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:53,319
under the right facts, become an open trunk on

76
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:54,967
the shoulder of the highway.

77
00:02:55,140 --> 00:02:57,179
Not everyone agreed at the time.

78
00:02:57,180 --> 00:03:01,139
Justice McReynolds, joined by Justice Sutherland,
dissented.

79
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:04,339
But the majority held —
and here's the phrase to hold onto,

80
00:03:04,340 --> 00:03:07,619
because the entire story turns on it.

81
00:03:07,620 --> 00:03:08,919
Probable cause.

82
00:03:08,920 --> 00:03:10,559
Not a warrant.
Not certainty.

83
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:11,799
But not a hunch either.

84
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:16,259
We're going to see exactly where they bite.

85
00:03:16,260 --> 00:03:19,139
Here's the part movies and TV get wrong.

86
00:03:19,140 --> 00:03:21,519
The automobile exception is not a blank check.

87
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:24,299
Being pulled over is not, by itself,

88
00:03:24,300 --> 00:03:26,065
permission to search your car.

89
00:03:26,120 --> 00:03:28,460
And a hunch is not enough.

90
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:31,219
Probable cause means a fair,

91
00:03:31,220 --> 00:03:33,499
fact-based probability that there's

92
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:37,919
contraband or evidence inside —
something an officer can point to and articulate,

93
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:39,120
not just a feeling.

94
00:03:39,420 --> 00:03:41,773
A busted taillight does not, on its own,

95
00:03:42,440 --> 00:03:45,459
get anyone into your trunk.
So what does clear the bar?

96
00:03:45,460 --> 00:03:48,279
The smell of something illegal in some places.

97
00:03:48,280 --> 00:03:49,679
Something an officer sees in

98
00:03:49,680 --> 00:03:51,879
plain view on the seat.

99
00:03:51,880 --> 00:03:53,059
An admission.

100
00:03:53,060 --> 00:03:54,859
A trained dog's alert.

101
00:03:54,860 --> 00:03:57,819
Facts that add up.
And once that line is crossed —

102
00:03:57,820 --> 00:04:00,179
once there really is probable cause —

103
00:04:00,180 --> 00:04:03,299
the search can go much further than people expect.

104
00:04:03,300 --> 00:04:06,119
Because the Supreme Court has said the
reach of the search is defined

105
00:04:06,120 --> 00:04:08,479
by the thing they're looking for.

106
00:04:08,480 --> 00:04:10,279
If the object could fit in the glovebox,

107
00:04:10,280 --> 00:04:11,819
they can open the glovebox.

108
00:04:11,820 --> 00:04:13,039
If it could be in the trunk,

109
00:04:13,040 --> 00:04:14,739
they can open the trunk.

110
00:04:14,740 --> 00:04:16,039
If it could be inside a

111
00:04:16,040 --> 00:04:17,619
zipped bag on the back seat,

112
00:04:17,620 --> 00:04:20,440
they can open the bag.

113
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:24,999
Locked container, loose container —
if that's a place the thing could be,

114
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:25,759
it's fair game.

115
00:04:25,760 --> 00:04:27,899
That reach didn't come out of nowhere.

116
00:04:27,900 --> 00:04:30,919
It was settled in two Supreme Court decisions —

117
00:04:30,920 --> 00:04:33,519
United States v. Ross in 1982,

118
00:04:33,520 --> 00:04:35,999
and California v. Acevedo in 1991 —

119
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:38,479
which held that a closed bag or a locked box

120
00:04:38,480 --> 00:04:41,879
inside the car gets no special shield of its own.

121
00:04:41,880 --> 00:04:43,739
If the thing they have probable cause

122
00:04:43,740 --> 00:04:47,199
to find could be in there, they can open it.

123
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:49,379
But that same rule cuts the other way,

124
00:04:49,380 --> 00:04:52,399
and it's the closest thing you have to a leash.

125
00:04:52,400 --> 00:04:54,799
The search has to fit the reason for it.

126
00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:56,119
If the probable cause is

127
00:04:56,120 --> 00:04:58,319
about a stolen fifty-inch television,

128
00:04:58,320 --> 00:05:01,839
that does not justify opening a tiny pill bottle —

129
00:05:01,840 --> 00:05:03,779
a TV can't hide in there.

130
00:05:03,780 --> 00:05:06,459
The bigger the thing they're chasing,

131
00:05:06,460 --> 00:05:08,779
the fewer the places they're allowed to look.

132
00:05:08,780 --> 00:05:09,519
The smaller it is,

133
00:05:09,520 --> 00:05:11,599
the more of your car opens up.

134
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:14,479
They don't even have to
prove a separate emergency, though.

135
00:05:14,480 --> 00:05:15,939
The Court has been blunt about it:

136
00:05:15,940 --> 00:05:19,600
if the car is drivable and there's probable cause,

137
00:05:20,660 --> 00:05:21,239
that's enough —

138
00:05:21,240 --> 00:05:22,339
no extra excuse required.

139
00:05:22,340 --> 00:05:24,419
The Justices spelled that out

140
00:05:24,420 --> 00:05:26,399
again in the nineteen-nineties:

141
00:05:26,400 --> 00:05:30,600
a car that's readily mobile, plus probable cause,

142
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:31,879
needs nothing more.

143
00:05:31,880 --> 00:05:32,579
No ticking clock.

144
00:05:32,580 --> 00:05:35,019
No proof the driver was about to speed off.

145
00:05:35,020 --> 00:05:37,999
The car being a car is the emergency.

146
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,519
Now step back and feel what that means for you.

147
00:05:40,520 --> 00:05:44,419
Your car is where you keep your bag, your phone,

148
00:05:44,420 --> 00:05:45,119
your paperwork,

149
00:05:45,120 --> 00:05:48,279
half of your private life while it's in motion.

150
00:05:48,280 --> 00:05:50,039
And it carries far thinner

151
00:05:50,040 --> 00:05:51,439
protection than your living room —

152
00:05:51,440 --> 00:05:53,799
for a reason written down

153
00:05:53,800 --> 00:05:55,279
before your grandparents were born.

154
00:05:55,280 --> 00:05:57,809
Which raises the only question that's left.

155
00:05:58,260 --> 00:05:59,639
If the car is this exposed —

156
00:05:59,640 --> 00:06:03,719
is there anywhere the police

157
00:06:03,720 --> 00:06:06,879
still cannot follow it?
To find the line, go to Virginia —

158
00:06:06,880 --> 00:06:09,859
and a motorcycle that kept getting away.

159
00:06:09,860 --> 00:06:11,199
An officer had twice been outrun

160
00:06:11,200 --> 00:06:13,219
by the same distinctive orange-and-black bike,

161
00:06:13,220 --> 00:06:15,039
and he suspected it was stolen.

162
00:06:15,040 --> 00:06:17,079
Then, one day, he tracked it down.

163
00:06:17,080 --> 00:06:19,419
Not on a street.

164
00:06:19,420 --> 00:06:20,059
To a house.

165
00:06:20,060 --> 00:06:21,520
And there it was:

166
00:06:21,900 --> 00:06:22,939
parked in the driveway,

167
00:06:22,940 --> 00:06:25,059
pulled up close to the side of the home,

168
00:06:25,060 --> 00:06:27,659
with a tarp thrown over it.

169
00:06:27,660 --> 00:06:29,895
So the officer walked up the driveway.

170
00:06:30,280 --> 00:06:33,179
He lifted the tarp.
He ran the plate and the vehicle number —

171
00:06:33,180 --> 00:06:34,959
and it came back stolen.

172
00:06:34,960 --> 00:06:37,859
Then he waited for the man staying there,

173
00:06:37,860 --> 00:06:39,999
Ryan Collins, and arrested him.

174
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,779
Here's why this case is the whole ballgame.

175
00:06:42,780 --> 00:06:44,019
The officer had real reason to

176
00:06:44,020 --> 00:06:46,599
think that bike was stolen —

177
00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:47,459
that's probable cause.

178
00:06:47,460 --> 00:06:48,799
And if that motorcycle had been

179
00:06:48,800 --> 00:06:50,679
sitting on a public street,

180
00:06:50,680 --> 00:06:55,219
lifting the tarp is a clean, legal,

181
00:06:55,220 --> 00:06:57,398
automobile-exception search.
So Virginia argued:

182
00:06:57,580 --> 00:06:58,779
same thing here.

183
00:06:58,780 --> 00:07:00,939
A vehicle, probable cause, done —

184
00:07:00,940 --> 00:07:03,499
it shouldn't matter that it was in a driveway.

185
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:04,959
But think about how much that

186
00:07:04,960 --> 00:07:06,739
position was really asking for.

187
00:07:06,740 --> 00:07:08,099
To search a car parked

188
00:07:08,100 --> 00:07:09,999
at a stranger's public curb,

189
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:13,239
an officer needs nothing but probable cause.

190
00:07:13,240 --> 00:07:14,939
Virginia wanted that same power to

191
00:07:14,940 --> 00:07:17,259
include walking onto private property —

192
00:07:17,260 --> 00:07:18,319
up the driveway,

193
00:07:18,320 --> 00:07:20,679
past the point where a home begins.

194
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:22,139
If that were allowed,

195
00:07:22,140 --> 00:07:25,419
the most exposed thing you own, your car,

196
00:07:25,420 --> 00:07:27,119
could become the key that unlocks

197
00:07:27,120 --> 00:07:30,939
the most protected place you have, your home.

198
00:07:30,940 --> 00:07:34,099
The Supreme Court said no.

199
00:07:34,100 --> 00:07:36,199
In 2018, by a vote of eight to one,

200
00:07:36,200 --> 00:07:38,399
the Court held that the car-search power does not

201
00:07:38,400 --> 00:07:41,259
reach onto the ground that belongs to your home.

202
00:07:41,260 --> 00:07:43,084
There's a word for that ground.

203
00:07:43,500 --> 00:07:45,239
The porch, the front steps,

204
00:07:45,240 --> 00:07:48,339
the strip of driveway pressed against the walls —

205
00:07:48,340 --> 00:07:50,599
the law calls it the curtilage.

206
00:07:50,600 --> 00:07:52,819
And curtilage is treated as part of the home.

207
00:07:52,820 --> 00:07:55,459
It gets the home's full protection.

208
00:07:55,460 --> 00:07:57,479
And that isn't a technicality.

209
00:07:57,480 --> 00:07:58,699
The home is the ground the

210
00:07:58,700 --> 00:08:00,919
Fourth Amendment guards most fiercely.

211
00:08:00,920 --> 00:08:02,579
The Court's point was simple:

212
00:08:02,580 --> 00:08:04,019
you cannot let a rule built for

213
00:08:04,020 --> 00:08:05,999
the open road quietly swallow the

214
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:08,139
most protected ground a person owns.

215
00:08:08,140 --> 00:08:10,519
Where you park does not shrink your home.

216
00:08:10,520 --> 00:08:13,880
Your home enlarges what it protects.

217
00:08:14,140 --> 00:08:18,059
Justice Sotomayor, writing for the Court,
said it in one line worth keeping:

218
00:08:18,060 --> 00:08:23,599
"The scope of the automobile exception extends

219
00:08:23,600 --> 00:08:25,619
no further than the automobile itself."

220
00:08:25,620 --> 00:08:27,179
So both things are true at once.

221
00:08:27,180 --> 00:08:29,919
Out on the street, that same search is fine.

222
00:08:29,920 --> 00:08:32,060
But the instant the car is tucked

223
00:08:32,580 --> 00:08:34,639
into the protected ground hugging your house,

224
00:08:34,640 --> 00:08:37,179
the hundred-year exception runs out of road.

225
00:08:37,180 --> 00:08:39,799
To step onto that ground and search,

226
00:08:39,800 --> 00:08:42,479
police need a warrant —

227
00:08:42,480 --> 00:08:44,679
or a completely different exception.

228
00:08:44,680 --> 00:08:47,599
Only Justice Alito dissented —
worried the Court was letting a

229
00:08:47,600 --> 00:08:49,541
technicality shield a bike he had

230
00:08:49,840 --> 00:08:51,519
strong reason to believe was stolen.

231
00:08:51,520 --> 00:08:53,539
But he stood alone.

232
00:08:53,540 --> 00:08:56,839
The line held.
So now you know where the line falls.

233
00:08:56,840 --> 00:08:58,499
The question that actually matters is what

234
00:08:58,500 --> 00:09:00,640
it means the next time it's your

235
00:09:03,340 --> 00:09:05,499
window the officer is walking up to.

236
00:09:05,500 --> 00:09:07,379
So here's the actual map of

237
00:09:07,380 --> 00:09:10,079
your rights on the road —

238
00:09:10,080 --> 00:09:11,339
the one almost nobody is handed.

239
00:09:11,340 --> 00:09:13,739
On a public street or a routine stop,

240
00:09:13,740 --> 00:09:16,879
your car can be searched without a warrant —

241
00:09:16,880 --> 00:09:20,699
but only with probable cause,
and only as far as the thing

242
00:09:20,700 --> 00:09:22,899
they're looking for could actually be.

243
00:09:22,900 --> 00:09:25,719
Being arrested doesn't automatically
open the car either.

244
00:09:25,720 --> 00:09:27,099
In a case called Gant,

245
00:09:27,100 --> 00:09:29,479
the Court said that once you're handcuffed

246
00:09:29,480 --> 00:09:31,359
and set away from the vehicle,

247
00:09:31,360 --> 00:09:33,419
officers generally can't search it

248
00:09:33,420 --> 00:09:35,079
just because they arrested you —

249
00:09:35,080 --> 00:09:38,779
not unless it's reasonable to think evidence

250
00:09:38,780 --> 00:09:40,219
of that specific offense is inside.

251
00:09:40,220 --> 00:09:43,659
The protection can even follow you into a rental.

252
00:09:43,660 --> 00:09:45,479
The Court has said that if

253
00:09:45,480 --> 00:09:48,119
you're lawfully driving a rental car,

254
00:09:48,120 --> 00:09:51,219
you can keep a real expectation of privacy in it —

255
00:09:51,220 --> 00:09:54,599
even if your name isn't on the paperwork.

256
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:57,319
And there's one honest gray area worth naming.

257
00:09:57,320 --> 00:09:59,379
For years, in many places,

258
00:09:59,380 --> 00:10:00,839
the smell of marijuana alone

259
00:10:00,840 --> 00:10:02,419
was treated as probable cause.

260
00:10:02,420 --> 00:10:04,539
But the Supreme Court has never set

261
00:10:04,540 --> 00:10:07,799
a single nationwide rule on that —

262
00:10:07,800 --> 00:10:09,099
and in states that have

263
00:10:09,100 --> 00:10:10,924
legalized or decriminalized it,

264
00:10:11,060 --> 00:10:12,519
several courts now say the odor

265
00:10:12,520 --> 00:10:14,219
by itself isn't enough anymore.

266
00:10:14,220 --> 00:10:16,699
On that one, the honest answer is:

267
00:10:16,700 --> 00:10:19,959
it depends where you are.
So what does all of this mean

268
00:10:19,960 --> 00:10:21,959
the next time you're actually pulled over?

269
00:10:21,960 --> 00:10:24,559
You don't have to be a lawyer.

270
00:10:24,560 --> 00:10:27,059
Just carry the shape of it:

271
00:10:27,060 --> 00:10:29,379
they need a real, articulable reason —

272
00:10:29,380 --> 00:10:30,099
not a mood —

273
00:10:30,100 --> 00:10:31,519
before they can search;

274
00:10:31,520 --> 00:10:34,344
the search has to match the thing they're after;

275
00:10:34,980 --> 00:10:37,620
and the ground pressed against your
house is not the road.

276
00:10:38,220 --> 00:10:41,079
But the throughline is a century old,

277
00:10:41,080 --> 00:10:42,180
and it's simple.

278
00:10:42,740 --> 00:10:45,079
Your car trades privacy for mobility.

279
00:10:45,080 --> 00:10:46,419
The exception is broad —

280
00:10:46,420 --> 00:10:48,799
broader than most people believe —

281
00:10:48,800 --> 00:10:50,339
and it isn't a myth.

282
00:10:50,340 --> 00:10:53,879
It's just not infinite.

283
00:10:53,880 --> 00:10:56,519
It stops, exactly, at the edge of your home.

284
00:10:56,520 --> 00:10:58,639
If you drive, that line is yours —

285
00:10:58,640 --> 00:11:01,319
and most people go their whole lives

286
00:11:01,320 --> 00:11:03,439
never knowing exactly where it falls.

287
00:11:03,440 --> 00:11:06,699
If knowing where your rights actually stop changed

288
00:11:06,700 --> 00:11:09,779
how you'll see the next traffic stop, hit like —

289
00:11:09,780 --> 00:11:11,799
it honestly helps more drivers find this.

290
00:11:11,800 --> 00:11:13,179
And tell me in the comments:

291
00:11:13,180 --> 00:11:15,179
were you taught that police need

292
00:11:15,180 --> 00:11:18,591
a warrant to search your car?
Because for a hundred years, they haven't.
